LS.add({"dac416b214":{"t":"S26 seraphim 撒拉弗","b":1,"k":"unit","h":"<nav class=\"cr\" style=\"--c:#c0841a\"><a href=\"index.html\">燈臺 <i lang=\"en\">The Lampstand</i></a> <span class=\"sep\">›</span> <a class=\"fl0\" href=\"#ic52d1cf364\">1 原文研究 Original Languages</a> <span class=\"sep\">›</span> <a class=\"fl1\" href=\"#ie883cc6886\">1 詞彙 Vocabulary</a> <span class=\"sep\">›</span> <a class=\"fl2\" href=\"#i52badd311e\">Studies 研究</a></nav><h1 class=\"fl3\" id=\"s26--撒拉弗-seraphim\">S26 · <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">撒拉弗</span> seraphim</h1><details class=\"toc\"><summary>目錄 <i lang=\"en\">Contents</i></summary><ol><li class=\"l2\"><a href=\"#1-at-a-glance-%E4%B8%80%E8%A6%BD\">1. At a glance 一覽</a></li><li class=\"l2\"><a href=\"#2-root-and-family-%E5%AD%97%E6%A0%B9%E8%88%87%E5%AD%97%E6%97%8F\">2. Root and family 字根與字族</a></li><li class=\"l2\"><a href=\"#3-counts-and-significance-%E6%95%B8%E6%93%9A%E8%88%87%E6%84%8F%E7%BE%A9\">3. Counts and significance 數據與意義</a></li><li class=\"l3\"><a href=\"#1-totals-%E7%B8%BD%E6%95%B8\">1. Totals 總數</a></li><li class=\"l3\"><a href=\"#2-by-book-%E6%8C%89%E6%9B%B8%E5%8D%B7\">2. By book 按書卷</a></li><li class=\"l3\"><a href=\"#3-by-section-%E6%8C%89%E5%88%86%E6%AE%B5\">3. By section 按分段</a></li><li class=\"l3\"><a href=\"#4-by-grammatical-form-%E6%8C%89%E8%A9%9E%E5%BD%A2\">4. By grammatical form 按詞形</a></li><li class=\"l3\"><a href=\"#5-by-translation-%E6%8C%89%E8%AD%AF%E6%9C%AC\">5. By translation 按譯本</a></li><li class=\"l3\"><a href=\"#6-first-and-last-use-rank-hapax-%E9%A6%96%E6%AC%A1%E8%88%87%E6%9C%AB%E6%AC%A1%E6%8E%92%E5%90%8D%E7%BD%95%E7%94%A8\">6. First and last use; rank; hapax 首次與末次、排名、罕用</a></li><li class=\"l3\"><a href=\"#7-spread-%E5%88%86%E4%BD%88\">7. Spread 分佈</a></li><li class=\"l3\"><a href=\"#8-significance-%E6%84%8F%E7%BE%A9\">8. Significance 意義</a></li><li class=\"l2\"><a href=\"#4-%E5%92%8C%E5%90%88%E6%9C%AC-renderings-%E5%92%8C%E5%90%88%E6%9C%AC%E7%9A%84%E8%AD%AF%E6%B3%95-every-rendering-every-verse\">4. 和合本 renderings 和合本的譯法 (every rendering, every verse)</a></li><li class=\"l2\"><a href=\"#5-shades-of-meaning-%E6%84%8F%E7%BE%A9%E5%B1%A4%E6%AC%A1\">5. Shades of meaning 意義層次</a></li><li class=\"l2\"><a href=\"#6-the-lxx-bridge-%E4%B8%83%E5%8D%81%E5%A3%AB%E8%AD%AF%E6%9C%AC%E7%9A%84%E6%A9%8B%E6%A8%91\">6. The LXX bridge 七十士譯本的橋樑</a></li><li class=\"l2\"><a href=\"#7-companions-and-cross-references-%E5%90%8C%E4%BC%B4%E5%AD%97%E8%88%87%E4%BA%92%E5%8F%83\">7. Companions and cross-references 同伴字與互參</a></li><li class=\"l2\"><a href=\"#8-in-the-new-testament-%E6%96%B0%E7%B4%84%E7%9A%84%E7%94%A8%E6%B3%95\">8. In the New Testament 新約的用法</a></li><li class=\"l2\"><a href=\"#9-culture-and-background-%E6%96%87%E5%8C%96%E8%83%8C%E6%99%AF\">9. Culture and background 文化背景</a></li><li class=\"l2\"><a href=\"#10-theology-%E7%A5%9E%E5%AD%B8\">10. Theology 神學</a></li><li class=\"l2\"><a href=\"#12-for-practice-%E6%87%89%E7%94%A8\">11. For practice 應用</a></li><li class=\"l2\"><a href=\"#13-evaluation-%E8%A9%95%E4%BC%B0\">12. Evaluation 評估</a></li><li class=\"l2\"><a href=\"#14-sources-and-to-check-%E8%B3%87%E6%96%99%E8%88%87%E5%BE%85%E6%9F%A5\">13. Sources 資料</a></li></ol></details>\n<p><span class=\"off\">Angels <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">天使論</span></span> · <span class=\"off\">God <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">神論</span></span> · <span class=\"off\">Salvation <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">救恩論</span></span> · <span class=\"off\">Jesus Christ <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">基督論</span></span><span class=\"off\"></span></p>\n<h2 id=\"1-at-a-glance-一覽\">1. At a glance <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">一覽</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Hebrew:</strong> <big><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שָׂרָף</span></big> <em>sârâph</em> <span class=\"off\">H8314</span></p>\n<p><strong>Greek:</strong> –</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Seven times, two senses.</strong> <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שָׂרָף</span> stands 7 times in 7 verses, all in the Old Testament. Five name a snake: <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.6\">Num 21:6</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.8\">8</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Deut.8.15\">Deut 8:15</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.14.29\">Isa 14:29</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.30.6\">30:6</span>. Two name the beings above the throne: <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.2\">Isa 6:2</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.6\">6</span>. Those two verses are all the Bible says of seraphim by that name. Strong's has 'serpent, fiery serpent' and derives the noun 'from <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שָׂרַף</span> H8313', to burn. Whether the one word has one origin behind both senses is debated (section 10).</li>\n<li><strong>The wilderness.</strong> \"And Jehovah sent fiery serpents among the people, and they bit the people; and much people of Israel died.\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.6\">Num 21:6</span>, ASV) The Hebrew sets two nouns side by side, <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">הַ נְּחָשִׁ֣ים הַ שְּׂרָפִ֔ים</span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.6\">Num 21:6</span>, BHSA), and the <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">和合本</span> has <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">「於是耶和華使火蛇進入百姓中間，蛇就咬他們。」（</span><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.6\">Num 21:6</span><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">，和合本）</span>. Two verses later the noun stands alone for the thing Moses must make: \"Make thee a fiery serpent, and set it upon a standard\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.8\">Num 21:8</span>, ASV) (<span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">עֲשֵׂ֤ה לְךָ֙ שָׂרָ֔ף</span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.8\">Num 21:8</span>, BHSA)). Moses makes it in bronze, \"And Moses made a serpent of brass\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.9\">Num 21:9</span>, ASV) (<span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">וַ יַּ֤עַשׂ מֹשֶׁה֙ נְחַ֣שׁ נְחֹ֔שֶׁת</span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.9\">Num 21:9</span>, BHSA)). Moses recalls that \"great and terrible wilderness, wherein were fiery serpents and scorpions\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Deut.8.15\">Deut 8:15</span>, ASV).</li>\n<li><strong>The object destroyed.</strong> The word <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שָׂרָף</span> does not stand at <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"2Kgs.18.4\">2 Kgs 18:4</span>, but the object does: Hezekiah \"brake in pieces the brazen serpent that Moses had made; for unto those days the children of Israel did burn incense to it; and he called it Nehushtan\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"2Kgs.18.4\">2 Kgs 18:4</span>, ASV). A sign given for healing had become an object of worship.</li>\n<li><strong>Above the throne.</strong> \"In the year that king Uzziah died I saw the Lord sitting upon a throne, high and lifted up; and his train filled the temple.\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.1\">Isa 6:1</span>, ASV) Then \"Above him stood the seraphim: each one had six wings; with twain he covered his face, and with twain he covered his feet, and with twain he did fly.\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.2\">Isa 6:2</span>, ASV) (<span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שְׂרָפִ֨ים עֹמְדִ֤ים׀ מִ מַּ֨עַל֙ לֹ֔ו</span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.2\">Isa 6:2</span>, BHSA); the <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">和合本 「其上有撒拉弗侍立，各有六個翅膀：用兩個翅膀遮臉，兩個翅膀遮腳，兩個翅膀飛翔；」（</span><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.2\">Isa 6:2</span><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">，和合本）</span>). The noun comes first in the verse and has no article. Nothing is said of their number, and of their form only faces, feet, wings and, at verse 6, a hand.</li>\n<li><strong>What they say.</strong> \"And one cried unto another, and said, Holy, holy, holy, is Jehovah of hosts: the whole earth is full of his glory.\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.3\">Isa 6:3</span>, ASV) (<span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">קָדֹ֧ושׁ׀ קָדֹ֛ושׁ קָדֹ֖ושׁ יְהוָ֣ה צְבָאֹ֑ות</span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.3\">Isa 6:3</span>, BHSA); <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">「彼此呼喊說：聖哉！聖哉！聖哉！萬軍之耶和華；他的榮光充滿全地！」（</span><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.3\">Isa 6:3</span><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">，和合本）</span>). The prophet's answer is about his mouth: \"because I am a man of unclean lips, and I dwell in the midst of a people of unclean lips\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.5\">Isa 6:5</span>, ASV).</li>\n<li><strong>One of them flew.</strong> \"Then flew one of the seraphim unto me, having a live coal in his hand, which he had taken with the tongs from off the altar:\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.6\">Isa 6:6</span>, ASV) (<span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">וַ יָּ֣עָף אֵלַ֗י אֶחָד֙ מִן־ הַ שְּׂרָפִ֔ים</span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.6\">Isa 6:6</span>, BHSA)). The coal touches his lips, and the announcement follows: \"and thine iniquity is taken away, and thy sin forgiven.\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.7\">Isa 6:7</span>, ASV) (<span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">וְ סָ֣ר עֲוֹנֶ֔ךָ וְ חַטָּאתְךָ֖ תְּכֻפָּֽר׃</span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.7\">Isa 6:7</span>, BHSA)). Then the prophet hears \"Whom shall I send, and who will go for us?\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.8\">Isa 6:8</span>, ASV).</li>\n<li><strong>The flying serpent.</strong> Isaiah alone uses both senses. Philistia is warned, \"and his fruit shall be a fiery flying serpent\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.14.29\">Isa 14:29</span>, ASV) (<span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">וּ פִרְיֹ֖ו שָׂרָ֥ף מְעֹופֵֽף׃</span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.14.29\">Isa 14:29</span>, BHSA)), and the road to Egypt runs through a land of \"the viper and fiery flying serpent\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.30.6\">Isa 30:6</span>, ASV) (<span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">אֶפְעֶה֙ וְ שָׂרָ֣ף מְעֹופֵ֔ף</span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.30.6\">Isa 30:6</span>, BHSA)). The verb of flying in those two phrases is the verb used of the seraphim at <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.2\">Isa 6:2</span> and 6:6 (<span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">עוּף</span>, H5774).</li>\n<li><strong>Not in the New Testament.</strong> Neither the word nor a Greek form of it stands there. The serpent returns as <span lang=\"grc\">ὄφις</span>: \"And as Moses lifted up the serpent in the wilderness, even so must the Son of man be lifted up;\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"John.3.14\">John 3:14</span>, ASV). The cry returns from other beings: \"And the four living creatures, having each one of them six wings\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rev.4.8\">Rev 4:8</span>, ASV), who say \"Holy, holy, holy, is the Lord God, the Almighty\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rev.4.8\">Rev 4:8</span>, ASV). And John says of Isaiah, \"These things said Isaiah, because he saw his glory; and he spake of him.\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"John.12.41\">John 12:41</span>, ASV)</li>\n<li><strong>Where it stands (log-likelihood):</strong> Isaiah 4 of the 7 (G² 14), Numbers 2, Deuteronomy once. By section: the Law 3, the Major Prophets 4 (G² 4). With so few occurrences these figures describe where the word stands and little more.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">看圖</span> See it</strong> — the counts by book and by section are the two charts of section 3; the renderings with their counts are sections 3.7 and 4:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bible circles <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">聖經圓圖</span>: <span class=\"off\">Numbers 21, the serpents and the standard</span> · <span class=\"off\">Deuteronomy 8, the wilderness recalled</span> · <span class=\"off\">Isaiah 6, the throne</span> · <span class=\"off\">Isaiah 14, the flying serpent</span> · <span class=\"off\">Isaiah 30, the road to Egypt</span> · <span class=\"off\">John 3, the lifting up</span> · <span class=\"off\">Revelation 4, the living creatures</span> · the app itself: <span class=\"off\">the circles (file)</span></li>\n<li>People (the circles' people, four languages): <span class=\"off\"><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">摩西</span> Moses <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">מֹשֶׁה</span> <span lang=\"grc\">Μωϋσῆς</span></span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.8\">Num 21:8</span>) · <span class=\"off\"><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">烏西雅</span> Uzziah <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">עֻזִּיָּה</span> <span lang=\"grc\">Ὀζίας</span></span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.1\">Isa 6:1</span>) · <span class=\"off\"><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">以賽亞</span> Isaiah <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">יְשַׁעְיָה</span> <span lang=\"grc\">Ἠσαΐας</span></span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.1\">Isa 6:1</span>) · <span class=\"off\"><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">希西家</span> Hezekiah <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">חִזְקִיָּהוּ</span> <span lang=\"grc\">Ἑζεκίας</span></span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"2Kgs.18.4\">2 Kgs 18:4</span>) · <span class=\"off\"><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">耶穌</span> Jesus <span lang=\"grc\">Ἰησοῦς</span></span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"John.3.14\">John 3:14</span>) · <span class=\"off\"><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">約翰</span> John <span lang=\"grc\">Ἰωάννης</span></span> (Rev 4) · <span class=\"off\"><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">保羅</span> Paul <span lang=\"grc\">Παῦλος</span></span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"1Cor.10.9\">1 Cor 10:9</span>)</li>\n<li>Places: <span class=\"off\"><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">以東</span> Edom <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">אֱדֹם</span></span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.4\">Num 21:4</span>) · <span class=\"off\"><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">埃及</span> Egypt <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">מִצְרַיִם</span> <span lang=\"grc\">Αἴγυπτος</span></span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.5\">Num 21:5</span>; Isa 30) · <span class=\"off\"><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">耶路撒冷</span> Jerusalem <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">יְרוּשָׁלַ</span>͏<span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">ִם</span> <span lang=\"grc\">Ἰερουσαλήμ</span></span> (Isa 6) · <span class=\"off\">the Holy Land in 3D <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">聖地立體地圖</span></span> (the map has no place links yet: find them by name)</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote class=\"verse\"><div class=\"zh\"><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">於是耶和華使火蛇進入百姓中間，蛇就咬他們。以色列人中死了許多。（<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.6\">民數記 21:6</span>）</span></div><div class=\"en\">And Jehovah sent fiery serpents among the people, and they bit the people; and much people of Israel died. (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.6\">Numbers 21:6</span>, ASV)</div></blockquote>\n<blockquote class=\"verse\"><div class=\"zh\"><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">耶和華對摩西說：「你製造一條火蛇，掛在杆子上；凡被咬的，一望這蛇，就必得活。」（<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.8\">民數記 21:8</span>）</span></div><div class=\"en\">And Jehovah said unto Moses, Make thee a fiery serpent, and set it upon a standard: and it shall come to pass, that every one that is bitten, when he seeth it, shall live. (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.8\">Numbers 21:8</span>, ASV)</div></blockquote>\n<blockquote class=\"verse\"><div class=\"zh\"><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">其上有撒拉弗侍立，各有六個翅膀：用兩個翅膀遮臉，兩個翅膀遮腳，兩個翅膀飛翔；（<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.2\">以賽亞書 6:2</span>）</span></div><div class=\"en\">Above him stood the seraphim: each one had six wings; with twain he covered his face, and with twain he covered his feet, and with twain he did fly. (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.2\">Isaiah 6:2</span>, ASV)</div></blockquote>\n<blockquote class=\"verse\"><div class=\"zh\"><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">彼此呼喊說：聖哉！聖哉！聖哉！萬軍之耶和華；他的榮光充滿全地！（<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.3\">以賽亞書 6:3</span>）</span></div><div class=\"en\">And one cried unto another, and said, Holy, holy, holy, is Jehovah of hosts: the whole earth is full of his glory. (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.3\">Isaiah 6:3</span>, ASV)</div></blockquote>\n<blockquote class=\"verse\"><div class=\"zh\"><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">有一撒拉弗飛到我跟前，手裏拿着紅炭，是用火剪從壇上取下來的，（<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.6\">以賽亞書 6:6</span>）</span></div><div class=\"en\">Then flew one of the seraphim unto me, having a live coal in his hand, which he had taken with the tongs from off the altar: (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.6\">Isaiah 6:6</span>, ASV)</div></blockquote>\n<blockquote class=\"verse\"><div class=\"zh\"><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">將炭沾我的口，說：「看哪，這炭沾了你的嘴，你的罪孽便除掉，你的罪惡就赦免了。」（<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.7\">以賽亞書 6:7</span>）</span></div><div class=\"en\">and he touched my mouth with it, and said, Lo, this hath touched thy lips; and thine iniquity is taken away, and thy sin forgiven. (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.7\">Isaiah 6:7</span>, ASV)</div></blockquote>\n<h2 id=\"2-root-and-family-字根與字族\">2. Root and family <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">字根與字族</span> <a class=\"up\" href=\"#1-at-a-glance-%E4%B8%80%E8%A6%BD\" title=\"回到摘要 back to the summary\">↑</a></h2>\n<p><strong>Root <big><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שָׂרַף</span></big> <em>sâraph</em> <span class=\"off\">H8313</span></strong> — a primitive root</p>\n<p><strong>Stems <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">字幹</span> (binyanim) of <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שָׂרַף</span> <em>sâraph</em> <span class=\"off\">H8313</span></strong> — 117 of the 117 words tagged H8313 are verb forms (BHSA; participles and infinitives included), in the grammar sheets' order:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Stem <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">字幹</span></th><th>Meaning <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">意思</span></th><th>Times</th><th>Share</th><th>First use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Qal (<span class=\"off\">page 2 <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">圖</span></span>)</td><td>simple active <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">簡單主動</span></td><td>102</td><td>87%</td></tr><tr><td>Niphal (<span class=\"off\">page 2 <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">圖</span></span>)</td><td>simple passive or reflexive <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">簡單被動或反身</span></td><td>14</td><td>12%</td></tr><tr><td>Piel (<span class=\"off\">page 2 <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">圖</span></span>)</td><td>intensive active <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">加強主動</span></td><td>–</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Pual (<span class=\"off\">page 2 <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">圖</span></span>)</td><td>intensive passive <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">加強被動</span></td><td>1</td><td>&lt;1%</td></tr><tr><td>Hiphil (<span class=\"off\">page 2 <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">圖</span></span>)</td><td>causative active <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">使役主動</span></td><td>–</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Hophal (<span class=\"off\">page 2 <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">圖</span></span>)</td><td>causative passive <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">使役被動</span></td><td>–</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Hithpael (<span class=\"off\">page 2 <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">圖</span></span>)</td><td>intensive reflexive <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">加強反身</span></td><td>–</td><td></td></tr><tr><td><strong>All stems</strong></td><td></td><td><strong>117</strong></td><td>100%</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Built on it (Strong's data)</th><th>Gloss</th><th>Times</th><th>In this study</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שְׂרֵפָה</span> <em>sᵉrêphâh</em> <span class=\"off\">H8316</span></td><td>burning</td><td>13</td><td></td></tr><tr><td><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">רֶשֶׁף</span> <em>resheph</em> <span class=\"off\">H7565</span></td><td>flame</td><td>7</td><td></td></tr><tr><td><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שָׂרָף</span> <em>sârâph</em> <span class=\"off\">H8314</span></td><td>serpent</td><td>7</td><td>✓</td></tr><tr><td><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">מִשְׂרָפָה</span> <em>misrâphâh</em> <span class=\"off\">H4955</span></td><td>burning</td><td>2</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><em>The whole family (Strong's data, every level):</em> 5 words — the 4 above and 1 built on them.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שָׂרָף</span> (H8314): Strong's 'from <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שָׂרַף</span> H8313', 'serpent, fiery serpent', with the King James renderings 'fiery (serpent), seraph.' Strong's has one entry for both senses. BDB has two. Its first noun is 'a serpent, usually venomous', with the name explained, cautiously, 'possibly from above √', from the 'burning effect of poison', and at <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.14.29\">Isa 14:29</span> and 30:6 'a flying serpent, or dragon'. Its second, in the plural, is seraphim, 'majestic beings with six wings, and human hands and voices', and for that noun BDB gives the comparisons taken up in section 10.</li>\n<li>UBS follows the same division in three glosses: 'carpet viper', with the note that it is 'easily provoked and quite aggressive' (it names Echis coloratus); 'seraph', 'heavenly creature with six wings', 'surrounding the throne of God and praising his holiness'; and 'Saraph', a man of Judah. ISBE (William Owen Carver, SERAPHIM): 'The origin of the term in Heb is uncertain'.</li>\n<li>The forms (BHSA, counted 8 Nov): singular absolute 4 (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.8\">Num 21:8</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Deut.8.15\">Deut 8:15</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.14.29\">Isa 14:29</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.30.6\">30:6</span>), plural absolute 3 (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.6\">Num 21:6</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.2\">Isa 6:2</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.6\">6:6</span>). The article stands at <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.6\">Num 21:6</span> and <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.6\">Isa 6:6</span>. Every singular is a snake. Every use for the beings is plural, and one plural is a snake (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.6\">Num 21:6</span>).</li>\n<li><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שָׂרַף</span> (H8313), the verb 'to burn', 117 times in 107 verses, Strong's 'a primitive root'. BDB compares Assyrian šarâpu. Strong's derives three other nouns from it: <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שְׂרֵפָה</span> (H8316, BDB 'burning', 13 times), <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">מִשְׂרָפָה</span> (H4955, 'a burning'), and <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">רֶשֶׁף</span> (H7565, 'flame, firebolt, spark'). BDB does not arrange <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">רֶשֶׁף</span> under this root, so that last derivation is Strong's alone.</li>\n<li><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שָׂרָף</span> (H8315), a man's name, once: \"and Joash, and Saraph, who had dominion in Moab\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"1Chr.4.22\">1 Chr 4:22</span>, ASV) (<span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">וְ שָׂרָ֛ף</span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"1Chr.4.22\">1 Chr 4:22</span>, BHSA); the <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">和合本 「約阿施、薩拉」（</span><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"1Chr.4.22\">1 Chr 4:22</span><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">，和合本）</span>). BDB 'a Judahite', and it reports that the Greek witnesses differ (<span lang=\"grc\">Σαια</span>, and <span lang=\"grc\">Σαραφ</span> in others). Strong's calls it 'the same as <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שָׂרָף</span> H8314'. The passage does not say why he bore the name.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"3-counts-and-significance-數據與意義\">3. Counts and significance <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">數據與意義</span> <a class=\"up\" href=\"#1-at-a-glance-%E4%B8%80%E8%A6%BD\" title=\"回到摘要 back to the summary\">↑</a></h2>\n<p><em>Sources: OT = BHSA (ETCBC words, prefixes count as words); NT = OpenGNT 3.3; KJV and ASV = eBible texts with Strong's tags; <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">和合本</span> = the word-by-word alignments (OpenHebrewBible, OpenGNT). Tests: log-likelihood G² with 1 degree of freedom.</em></p>\n<h3 id=\"1-totals-總數\">1. Totals <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">總數</span></h3>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Lemma</th><th>Testament</th><th>Words</th><th>Verses</th><th>Forms</th><th>Commonest forms</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שָׂרָף</span> <em>sârâph</em> <span class=\"off\">H8314</span></td><td>OT</td><td>7</td><td>7</td><td>6</td><td><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שְּׂרָפִ֔ים</span> 2, <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שָׂרָ֔ף</span> 1, <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שָׂרָף֙</span> 1</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Hebrew seraph, fiery serpent <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שָׂרָף</span> family</strong></td><td>OT</td><td><strong>7</strong></td><td><strong>7</strong></td><td>6</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Whole Bible:</strong> 7 Hebrew words + 0 Greek words = 7 (the two languages are counted in their own texts; the Septuagint is not added).</p>\n<p><strong>Lexeme check (Strong's tag against the BHSA lexeme):</strong> every word counted carries the same BHSA lexeme as its Strong's number.</p>\n<h3 id=\"2-by-book-按書卷\">2. By book <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">按書卷</span></h3>\n<p><img class=\"emb\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"charts/S26 seraphim - books.svg\" src=\"pages/5e622cc00c.svg\"></p>\n<p><strong>Hebrew seraph, fiery serpent <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שָׂרָף</span>.</strong> Most uses: Isa 4, Num 2, Deut 1. Highest rate (2 or more uses): Isa 1.7, Num 0.9.</p>\n<p><strong>Keyness:</strong> Isa (G² 14, p &lt; 0.001, 10.0× expected).</p>\n<details class=\"callout c-table\"><summary class=\"ctitle\">Hebrew seraph, fiery serpent <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שָׂרָף</span>: every book (count, rate, expected, G²)</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><div class=\"tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Book</th><th>Count</th><th>Per 10,000</th><th>Expected</th><th>G²</th><th>p</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Num <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">民數記</span></td><td>2</td><td>0.86</td><td>0.4</td><td>3.8</td><td>not significant</td></tr><tr><td>Deut <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">申命記</span></td><td>1</td><td>0.50</td><td>0.3</td><td>0.9</td><td>not significant</td></tr><tr><td>Isa <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">以賽亞書</span></td><td>4</td><td>1.74</td><td>0.4</td><td>14.2</td><td>p &lt; 0.001</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></details>\n<p><em>G² (log-likelihood) compares the count in a book with what the book's size predicts; G² ≥ 10.83 means p &lt; 0.001, a chance below 1 in 1,000 that the excess is luck.</em></p>\n<h3 id=\"3-by-section-按分段\">3. By section <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">按分段</span></h3>\n<p><img class=\"emb\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"charts/S26 seraphim - sections.svg\" src=\"pages/095e1bd04e.svg\"></p>\n<p><strong>Hebrew seraph, fiery serpent <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שָׂרָף</span></strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Count</th><th>Per 10,000</th><th>Expected</th><th>G²</th><th>p</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Law <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">律法書</span></td><td>3</td><td>0.27</td><td>1.9</td><td>0.9</td><td>not significant</td></tr><tr><td>History <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">歷史書</span></td><td>0</td><td>0.00</td><td>2.5</td><td>6.3</td><td>below</td></tr><tr><td>Wisdom and Poetry <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">詩歌智慧書</span></td><td>0</td><td>0.00</td><td>0.8</td><td>1.8</td><td>below</td></tr><tr><td>Major Prophets <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">大先知書</span></td><td>4</td><td>0.45</td><td>1.5</td><td>4.4</td><td>p &lt; 0.05</td></tr><tr><td>Minor Prophets <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">小先知書</span></td><td>0</td><td>0.00</td><td>0.3</td><td>0.7</td><td>below</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"4-by-grammatical-form-按詞形\">4. By grammatical form <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">按詞形</span></h3>\n<ul>\n<li><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שָׂרָף</span> <em>sârâph</em> <span class=\"off\">H8314</span> — <em>part of speech:</em> noun 7 · <em>number:</em> singular 4, plural 3 · <em>state:</em> absolute 7 · <em>pronoun suffix:</em> none 7</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"5-by-translation-按譯本\">5. By translation <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">按譯本</span></h3>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Lemma</th><th>KJV (its own tags)</th><th>ASV (machine-tagged)</th><th><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">和合本</span> (alignment; full list in section 4)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שָׂרָף</span> <em>sârâph</em> <span class=\"off\">H8314</span></td><td>fiery 4, serpent 3, seraphims 2</td><td>fiery 4, seraphim 2, fiery serpent 1, adder 1, serpent 1</td><td><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">一條火蛇 1, 撒拉弗 1, 是火焰 1, 有撒拉弗 1, 火 1, 那裡有火蛇</span> 1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><em>The KJV counts are its own Strong's tags. The ASV's tags were added by machine and sometimes sit on the wrong word, so its counts are a guide only; \"–\" = no tag found. Small words that carry a tag (and, of, the, for, let …) are left out of both lists.</em></p>\n<h3 id=\"6-first-and-last-use-rank-hapax-首次與末次排名罕用\">6. First and last use; rank; hapax <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">首次與末次、排名、罕用</span></h3>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Lemma</th><th>First</th><th>Last</th><th>Rank among all lemmas</th><th>Once only?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שָׂרָף</span> <em>sârâph</em> <span class=\"off\">H8314</span></td><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.6\">Num 21:6</span></td><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.30.6\">Isa 30:6</span></td><td>2784 of 8,563 (OT)</td><td>no</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"7-spread-分佈\">7. Spread <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">分佈</span></h3>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Lemma / family</th><th>Books with it</th><th>DP (0 = even, 1 = one corner)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שָׂרָף</span> <em>sârâph</em> <span class=\"off\">H8314</span></td><td>3 of 39</td><td>0.845</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Hebrew seraph, fiery serpent <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שָׂרָף</span> family</strong></td><td>3 of 39</td><td><strong>0.845</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><em>DP (Gries' deviation of proportions): how far the word's spread over the books is from what the book sizes predict.</em></p>\n<h3 id=\"8-significance-意義\">8. Significance <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">意義</span></h3>\n<p><strong>Statistical (log-likelihood G², chi-square with 1 df):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hebrew seraph, fiery serpent <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שָׂרָף</span>:</strong> sections over-represented: none at p &lt; 0.001; books: Isa (G² 14).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>In context (a judgment — the rubric of the template: F first mention · P programmatic or climactic · C covenant formula or God describing himself · Q quoted in the NT or quoting the OT · A carries the argument):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Passage</th><th>F</th><th>P</th><th>C</th><th>Q</th><th>A</th><th>Rating</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.6\">Num 21:6</span></td><td>F</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>★</td><td>first <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שָׂרָף</span>: \"And Jehovah sent fiery serpents among the people\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.6\">Num 21:6</span>, ASV)</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.8\">Num 21:8</span></td><td></td><td></td><td>C</td><td>Q</td><td></td><td>★★</td><td>the noun alone, the thing to be made: \"Make thee a fiery serpent, and set it upon a standard\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.8\">Num 21:8</span>, ASV)</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Deut.8.15\">Deut 8:15</span></td><td></td><td></td><td>C</td><td></td><td></td><td>★</td><td>recalled with scorpions: \"wherein were fiery serpents and scorpions\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Deut.8.15\">Deut 8:15</span>, ASV)</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.2\">Isa 6:2</span></td><td></td><td>P</td><td>C</td><td>Q</td><td></td><td>★★★</td><td>the beings, plural: \"Above him stood the seraphim: each one had six wings\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.2\">Isa 6:2</span>, ASV)</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.6\">Isa 6:6</span></td><td></td><td>P</td><td>C</td><td></td><td></td><td>★★</td><td>one of them, with a coal: \"Then flew one of the seraphim unto me\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.6\">Isa 6:6</span>, ASV)</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.14.29\">Isa 14:29</span></td><td></td><td></td><td>C</td><td></td><td></td><td>★</td><td>with the verb of flying, against Philistia: \"and his fruit shall be a fiery flying serpent\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.14.29\">Isa 14:29</span>, ASV)</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.30.6\">Isa 30:6</span></td><td>F</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>★</td><td>last <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שָׂרָף</span>, the road to Egypt: \"the viper and fiery flying serpent\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.30.6\">Isa 30:6</span>, ASV)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><em>Data behind \"A\" — where the words are densest (ten-verse stretches with three or more uses):</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hebrew seraph, fiery serpent <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שָׂרָף</span>: no dense stretch.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"4-和合本-renderings-和合本的譯法-every-rendering-every-verse\">4. <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">和合本</span> renderings <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">和合本的譯法</span> (every rendering, every verse) <a class=\"up\" href=\"#1-at-a-glance-%E4%B8%80%E8%A6%BD\" title=\"回到摘要 back to the summary\">↑</a></h2>\n<p><em>From the word-by-word alignments (OT: OpenHebrewBible CUV-to-BHS; NT: OpenGNT CUVtc). Pronouns and particles at the edges are set aside for grouping (<span lang=\"zh-Hant\">他的慈愛</span> is listed under <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">慈愛</span>, and the raw forms are kept). A use the alignment does not tie to a Chinese word is listed as \"not tied\".</em></p>\n<h4 id=\"שרף-sârâph-h8314--7-uses-7-renderings\"><big><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שָׂרָף</span></big> <em>sârâph</em> <span class=\"off\">H8314</span> — 7 uses, 7 renderings</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table><thead><tr><th><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">和合本</span></th><th>Count</th><th>Where (by section)</th><th>Forms in the text</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">一條火蛇</span></strong></td><td>1</td><td><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">律法書</span> 1</td><td><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">一條火蛇</span> 1</td></tr><tr><td><strong><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">撒拉弗</span></strong></td><td>1</td><td><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">大先知書</span> 1</td><td><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">撒拉弗</span> 1</td></tr><tr><td><strong><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">是火焰</span></strong></td><td>1</td><td><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">大先知書</span> 1</td><td><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">是火焰的</span> 1</td></tr><tr><td><strong><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">有撒拉弗</span></strong></td><td>1</td><td><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">大先知書</span> 1</td><td><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">有撒拉弗</span> 1</td></tr><tr><td><strong><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">火</span></strong></td><td>1</td><td><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">律法書</span> 1</td><td><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">火</span> 1</td></tr><tr><td><strong><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">那裡有火蛇</span></strong></td><td>1</td><td><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">律法書</span> 1</td><td><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">那裡有火蛇</span> 1</td></tr><tr><td><strong><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">龍</span></strong></td><td>1</td><td><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">大先知書</span> 1</td><td><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">龍</span> 1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<details class=\"callout c-list\"><summary class=\"ctitle\">Every verse, by rendering (H8314)</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p><strong><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">一條火蛇</span></strong> (1): <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.8\">Num 21:8</span><br><strong><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">撒拉弗</span></strong> (1): <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.6\">Isa 6:6</span><br><strong><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">是火焰</span></strong> (1): <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.14.29\">Isa 14:29</span><br><strong><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">有撒拉弗</span></strong> (1): <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.2\">Isa 6:2</span><br><strong><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">火</span></strong> (1): <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.6\">Num 21:6</span><br><strong><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">那裡有火蛇</span></strong> (1): <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Deut.8.15\">Deut 8:15</span><br><strong><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">龍</span></strong> (1): <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.30.6\">Isa 30:6</span></p></div></details>\n<p><strong>What the variety shows:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The counting engine aligns each occurrence to a separate string of characters and so lists 7 renderings of one occurrence each: <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">火</span> 1 (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.6\">Num 21:6</span>), <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">一條火蛇</span> 1 (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.8\">Num 21:8</span>), <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">那裡有火蛇</span> 1 (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Deut.8.15\">Deut 8:15</span>), <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">有撒拉弗</span> 1 (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.2\">Isa 6:2</span>), <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">撒拉弗</span> 1 (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.6\">Isa 6:6</span>), <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">是火焰</span> 1 (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.14.29\">Isa 14:29</span>) and <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">龍</span> 1 (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.30.6\">Isa 30:6</span>). The fragments are artefacts of aligning one Hebrew word with a Chinese phrase; the table in this section shows them as they fall.</li>\n<li>By string the <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">和合本</span> has three renderings (counted 8 Nov): <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">火蛇</span> in 3 verses (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.6\">Num 21:6</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.8\">8</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Deut.8.15\">Deut 8:15</span>), <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">撒拉弗</span> in 2 (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.2\">Isa 6:2</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.6\">6</span>) and <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">火焰的飛龍</span> in 2 (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.14.29\">Isa 14:29</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.30.6\">30:6</span>). So the translators divided what the Hebrew keeps as one noun, and in the two Isaiah oracles they added flame and called the creature a dragon: <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">「是火焰的飛龍」（</span><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.14.29\">Isa 14:29</span><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">，和合本）, 「蝮蛇、火焰的飛龍之地」（</span><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.30.6\">Isa 30:6</span><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">，和合本）</span>. The name in <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"1Chr.4.22\">1 Chr 4:22</span> is <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">薩拉</span>, and nothing links it visibly to the others.</li>\n<li>At <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.8-9\">Num 21:8–9</span> the <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">和合本</span> follows the Hebrew's change of word: <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">「你製造一條火蛇」（</span><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.8\">Num 21:8</span><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">，和合本）</span> and <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">「摩西便製造一條銅蛇」（</span><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.9\">Num 21:9</span><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">，和合本）</span>, where the Hebrew changes from <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שָׂרָף</span> to <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">נְחַ֣שׁ נְחֹ֔שֶׁת</span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.9\">Num 21:9</span>, BHSA), a serpent of bronze.</li>\n<li>English: the ASV has fiery serpents (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.6\">Num 21:6</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Deut.8.15\">Deut 8:15</span>), fiery serpent (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.8\">Num 21:8</span>), fiery flying serpent (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.14.29\">Isa 14:29</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.30.6\">30:6</span>) and seraphim (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.2\">Isa 6:2</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.6\">6</span>), counted by string 8 Nov. The KJV doubles the plural, \"Above it stood the seraphims\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.2\">Isa 6:2</span>, KJV), and at <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.14.29\">Isa 14:29</span> its cockatrice, \"shall come forth a cockatrice\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.14.29\">Isa 14:29</span>, KJV), renders <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">צֶפַע</span>, not this noun. Its pronoun at <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.2\">Isa 6:2</span> differs from the ASV's (section 10).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"5-shades-of-meaning-意義層次\">5. Shades of meaning <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">意義層次</span> <a class=\"up\" href=\"#1-at-a-glance-%E4%B8%80%E8%A6%BD\" title=\"回到摘要 back to the summary\">↑</a></h2>\n<p><em>UBS Dictionary of Biblical Hebrew (SDBH 0.9.2) and UBS Greek NT Dictionary 1.1: each sense with its domain and its verses (Hebrew verse numbers mapped to the English ones).</em></p>\n<p><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שָׂרָף</span> <em>sârâph</em> <span class=\"off\">H8314</span></strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Sense</th><th>Domain</th><th>Definition</th><th>Verses</th><th>For example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>carpet viper</td><td>Small Animals</td><td>= reddish brown viper with a pale underside and a row of pale blotches arranged side-by-side in pairs all the way down its back; it grows to about 80 …</td><td>5</td><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.6\">Num 21:6</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.8\">Num 21:8</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Deut.8.15\">Deut 8:15</span></td></tr><tr><td>seraph</td><td></td><td>= heavenly creature with six wings; ► surrounding the throne of God and praising his holiness</td><td>2</td><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.2\">Isa 6:2</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.6\">Isa 6:6</span></td></tr><tr><td>Saraph</td><td>Names of People</td><td>= man; ◄ tribe of Judah</td><td>1</td><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"1Chr.4.22\">1 Chr 4:22</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"6-the-lxx-bridge-七十士譯本的橋樑\">6. The LXX bridge <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">七十士譯本的橋樑</span> <a class=\"up\" href=\"#1-at-a-glance-%E4%B8%80%E8%A6%BD\" title=\"回到摘要 back to the summary\">↑</a></h2>\n<p><em>Verse by verse: each Hebrew verse beside its Septuagint verse. <strong>Matched</strong> = verses where the Hebrew and the Greek word are paired one to one, the strongest pair first, so a Greek word already explained by another Hebrew word in the verse is not counted again: the probable rendering, to read in the verses. <strong>Together</strong> = verses where both merely stand. <strong>× chance</strong> = together ÷ what the two verse counts predict. A row with Together but Matched 0 is a companion in the verse, not a rendering.</em></p>\n<p><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שָׂרָף</span> <em>sârâph</em> <span class=\"off\">H8314</span> → Greek</strong> (of 7 verses with a Septuagint verse)</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Greek in the LXX</th><th>Gloss</th><th>Matched</th><th>Together</th><th>× chance</th><th>For example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span lang=\"grc\">σεραφίμ</span> <span class=\"off\">G9067</span></td><td>seraphim</td><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>3321.3</td><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.6\">Isa 6:6</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.2\">Isa 6:2</span></td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"grc\">δάκνω</span> <em>dáknō</em> <span class=\"off\">G1143</span></td><td>to bite</td><td>1</td><td>3</td><td>830.3</td><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Deut.8.15\">Deut 8:15</span></td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"grc\">ὄφις</span> <em>óphis</em> <span class=\"off\">G3789</span></td><td>snake</td><td>1</td><td>4</td><td>474.5</td><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.8\">Num 21:8</span></td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"grc\">ἔκγονον</span> <em>ékgonon</em> <span class=\"off\">G1549</span></td><td>descendant</td><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>276.8</td><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.30.6\">Isa 30:6</span></td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"grc\">πέτομαι</span> <em>pétomai</em> <span class=\"off\">G4072</span></td><td>to fly</td><td>0</td><td>3</td><td>586.1</td><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.2\">Isa 6:2</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.30.6\">Isa 30:6</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.14.29\">Isa 14:29</span></td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"grc\">ἀσπίς</span> <em>aspís</em> <span class=\"off\">G785</span></td><td>asp</td><td>0</td><td>2</td><td>390.7</td><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.30.6\">Isa 30:6</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.14.29\">Isa 14:29</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>OT verses with these Hebrew words that the NT quotes</strong> (chart <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">舊約在新約</span> The Old Testament in the New, verse by verse: a list, or OpenBible where the tagged words have no list address, names the link, the NT words are tagged as an OT quotation (Levinsohn), and the two verses share words): none listed.</p>\n<p><strong>Also named as quoted or referenced</strong> (\"Old in New\", Nave: the lists do not sort allusion from quotation; kept only where these two verses share words or OpenBible readers link exactly these two verses): <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.8\">Num 21:8</span> → <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"John.3.14\">John 3:14</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.2\">Isa 6:2</span> → <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rev.4.8\">Rev 4:8</span>.</p>\n<p><em>Left out, too loose at verse level: 3 NT verses a list names only as part of a longer passage, without shared wording or reader links strong enough at that verse (all stand in the chart).</em></p>\n<p><em>Also 1 verses that stand inside a longer OT passage the NT refers to (for example Stephen's speech in Acts 7 or the warnings in Jude): <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.6\">Isa 6:6</span>.</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The LXX used here (Apostolic Bible Polyglot text, the Sixtine of 1587 by way of Bos 1709, not Rahlfs-Hanhart) never renders <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שָׂרָף</span> as fiery. In the two throne verses it carries the sound across: <em><span lang=\"grc\">και σεραφείμ ειστήκεισαν</span></em> (LXX <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.2\">Isa 6:2</span>, ABP), <em><span lang=\"grc\">εν των σεραφείμ</span></em> (LXX <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.6\">Isa 6:6</span>, ABP) (lemma <span lang=\"grc\">σεραφίμ</span>, the data's code G9067, 2 of the 7 verses; the lexicon line says 'Transliteration of Hebrew'). In the other five verses it uses snake words, and differently each time.</li>\n<li>The five: <em><span lang=\"grc\">τους όφεις τους θανατούντας</span></em> (LXX <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.6\">Num 21:6</span>, ABP), the deadly snakes; <em><span lang=\"grc\">ποίησον σεαυτώ όφιν χαλκούν</span></em> (LXX <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.8\">Num 21:8</span>, ABP), where the bronze of verse 9 is already named; <em><span lang=\"grc\">ου όφις δάκνων και σκορπίος</span></em> (LXX <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Deut.8.15\">Deut 8:15</span>, ABP), a biting snake; <em><span lang=\"grc\">εξελεύσονται όφεις πετώμενοι</span></em> (LXX <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.14.29\">Isa 14:29</span>, ABP), flying snakes; <em><span lang=\"grc\">και ασπίδες και έκγονα ασπίδων πετομένων</span></em> (LXX <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.30.6\">Isa 30:6</span>, ABP), asps and the offspring of flying asps. So the Greek reader would not connect the serpents with the seraphim by the word, and the two senses that stand together in Isaiah's Hebrew are separated in this Greek.</li>\n<li>Three differences in Isa 6. Position: the Hebrew has above, <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">מִ מַּ֨עַל֙ לֹ֔ו</span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.2\">Isa 6:2</span>, BHSA), and the LXX used here has round, <em><span lang=\"grc\">ειστήκεισαν κύκλω αυτού</span></em> (LXX <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.2\">Isa 6:2</span>, ABP). Movement: the Hebrew has one fly, <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">וַ יָּ֣עָף</span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.6\">Isa 6:6</span>, BHSA), and the LXX used here has one sent, <em><span lang=\"grc\">και απεστάλη προς με</span></em> (LXX <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.6\">Isa 6:6</span>, ABP). Glory: where the Hebrew says the skirts of his robe filled the temple, <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">וְ שׁוּלָ֖יו מְלֵאִ֥ים אֶת־ הַ הֵיכָֽל</span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.1\">Isa 6:1</span>, BHSA), the LXX used here has <em><span lang=\"grc\">και πλήρης ο οίκος της δόξης αυτού</span></em> (LXX <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.1\">Isa 6:1</span>, ABP), which bears on <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"John.12.41\">John 12:41</span> (section 8).</li>\n<li>The cry: <em><span lang=\"grc\">άγιος άγιος άγιος κύριος σαβαώθ πλήρης πάσα η γη της δόξης αυτού</span></em> (LXX <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.3\">Isa 6:3</span>, ABP), with the Hebrew title carried over as <span lang=\"grc\">σαβαώθ</span>. The forgiveness: <em><span lang=\"grc\">και αφελεί τας ανομίας σου και τας αμαρτίας σου περικαθαριεί</span></em> (LXX <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.7\">Isa 6:7</span>, ABP), future and active with no subject named, where the Hebrew has a perfect and a Pual (<span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">וְ סָ֣ר עֲוֹנֶ֔ךָ וְ חַטָּאתְךָ֖ תְּכֻפָּֽר׃</span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.7\">Isa 6:7</span>, BHSA)). The verb is the root of atonement, <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">כָּפַר</span> (H3722). The ASV says forgiven and the KJV purged: \"and thy sin purged\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.7\">Isa 6:7</span>, KJV).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"7-companions-and-cross-references-同伴字與互參\">7. Companions and cross-references <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">同伴字與互參</span> <a class=\"up\" href=\"#1-at-a-glance-%E4%B8%80%E8%A6%BD\" title=\"回到摘要 back to the summary\">↑</a></h2>\n<p><em>Words that stand with it far more often than chance. Same verse, and within two verses either side (same book). Ranked by G²; PMI = log₂(observed ÷ expected): 1 = twice as often as chance, 3 = eight times.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Hebrew seraph, fiery serpent <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שָׂרָף</span>, same verse:</strong> <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">עוּף</span> fly (4; PMI 8.78, G² 44), <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">נָחָשׁ</span> serpent (3; PMI 8.47, G² 31).</p>\n<p><strong>Hebrew seraph, fiery serpent <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שָׂרָף</span>, within two verses:</strong> <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">נָחָשׁ</span> serpent (4; PMI 6.78, G² 33), <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">עוּף</span> fly (4; PMI 6.53, G² 31), <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">עָשָׁן</span> smoke (3; PMI 6.46, G² 22), <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">סוּר</span> turn aside (3; PMI 2.95, G² 8), <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">צָבָא</span> service (3; PMI 2.54, G² 7), <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">מִצְרַיִם</span> Egypt (3; PMI 2.36, G² 6), <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">קָרָא</span> call (3; PMI 1.78, G² 4), <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שָׁלַח</span> send (3; PMI 1.67, G² 4), <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">עַם</span> people (4; PMI 1.2, G² 3), <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">יְהֹוָה</span> YHWH (6; PMI 0.64, G² 3).</p>\n<p><strong>Pairs: do the two words stand together?</strong> (verses with both; chance = what the two verse counts predict; G² for the 2 × 2 table of verses)</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שָׂרָף</span> + <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">נָחָשׁ</span>:</strong> 3 verses with both, chance alone 0.0 → more than chance, G² 31.1 (p &lt; 0.0001). <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.6\">Num 21:6</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Deut.8.15\">Deut 8:15</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.14.29\">Isa 14:29</span></li>\n<li><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שָׂרָף</span> + <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">עוּף</span>:</strong> 4 verses with both, chance alone 0.0 → more than chance, G² 44.2 (p &lt; 0.0001). <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.2\">Isa 6:2</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.6\">6:6</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.14.29\">14:29</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.30.6\">30:6</span></li>\n<li><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שָׂרָף</span> + <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">כָּנָף</span>:</strong> 1 verses with both, chance alone 0.0 → more than chance, G² 5.5 (p &lt; 0.05). <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.2\">Isa 6:2</span></li>\n<li>With serpent <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">נָחָשׁ</span>: 3 verses (chance alone 0.0). With flying <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">עוּף</span>: 4 verses (chance alone 0.0). With wing <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">כָּנָף</span>: 1 verses (chance alone 0.0). These figures describe seven verses and should be read as a description. The two senses share the flying verb in Isaiah only.</li>\n<li>A hand and a mouth. At <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.7\">Isa 6:7</span> the verb and preposition are those of Jeremiah's call: <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">וַ יַּגַּ֣ע עַל־ פִּ֔י</span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.7\">Isa 6:7</span>, BHSA) and <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">וַ יַּגַּ֖ע עַל־ פִּ֑י</span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Jer.1.9\">Jer 1:9</span>, BHSA), and there \"Then Jehovah put forth his hand, and touched my mouth\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Jer.1.9\">Jer 1:9</span>, ASV). In one the hand holds a coal, in the other the hand is Jehovah's and he gives words.</li>\n<li>Coals from the altar. \"And he shall take a censer full of coals of fire from off the altar before Jehovah\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Lev.16.12\">Lev 16:12</span>, ASV), on the Day of Atonement, the chapter of the mercy seat (S25), with another noun for coals, <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">גַּֽחֲלֵי־ אֵ֞שׁ</span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Lev.16.12\">Lev 16:12</span>, BHSA). <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.6\">Isa 6:6</span> has <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">רִצְפָּה</span>, and in both the fire comes from the altar.</li>\n<li>A cherub with fire. \"And the cherub stretched forth his hand from between the cherubim unto the fire that was between the cherubim, and took thereof, and put it into the hands of him that was clothed in linen\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ezek.10.7\">Ezek 10:7</span>, ASV), which is the nearest parallel to <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.6\">Isa 6:6</span>. There the fire goes out against the city (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ezek.10.2\">Ezek 10:2</span>); here it cleanses one man. Delitzsch builds a contrast on this, given in section 10.</li>\n<li>The serpent. Eden's <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">נָחָשׁ</span> is another word from <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שָׂרָף</span>: \"Now the serpent was more subtle than any beast of the field\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gen.3.1\">Gen 3:1</span>, ASV), and the New Testament's old serpent, \"the old serpent, he that is called the Devil and Satan\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rev.12.9\">Rev 12:9</span>, ASV), has no verbal link with Num 21 or Isa 6. <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.14.29\">Isa 14:29</span> sets the three snake words together: <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">כִּֽי־ מִ שֹּׁ֤רֶשׁ נָחָשׁ֙ יֵ֣צֵא צֶ֔פַע וּ פִרְיֹ֖ו שָׂרָ֥ף מְעֹופֵֽף׃</span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.14.29\">Isa 14:29</span>, BHSA).</li>\n<li>Verse cross-references: \"Old in New\" is used in section 6.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"8-in-the-new-testament-新約的用法\">8. In the New Testament <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">新約的用法</span> <a class=\"up\" href=\"#1-at-a-glance-%E4%B8%80%E8%A6%BD\" title=\"回到摘要 back to the summary\">↑</a></h2>\n<ul>\n<li>There is no New Testament word for seraphim. <span lang=\"grc\">σεραφίμ</span> (G9067) has no New Testament use in the local data. What the New Testament has is the serpent, the vision, and the cry.</li>\n<li>The serpent. <em><span lang=\"grc\">καὶ καθὼς Μωϋσῆς ὕψωσεν τὸν ὄφιν ἐν τῇ ἐρήμῳ οὕτως ὑψωθῆναι δεῖ τὸν Υἱὸν τοῦ ἀνθρώπου</span></em> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"John.3.14\">John 3:14</span>, OpenGNT), the <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">和合本 「摩西在曠野怎樣舉蛇，人子也必照樣被舉起來，」（</span><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"John.3.14\">John 3:14</span><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">，和合本）</span>. Jesus compares the lifting, and the verse does not call him a serpent or say that the serpent was a type of him (the positions: section 10). <span lang=\"grc\">ὄφις</span> stands 14 times in the New Testament.</li>\n<li>The tested Lord. <em><span lang=\"grc\">μηδὲ ἐκπειράζωμεν τὸν Χριστόν</span></em> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"1Cor.10.9\">1 Cor 10:9</span>, OpenGNT) recalls Num 21 (\"perished by the serpents\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"1Cor.10.9\">1 Cor 10:9</span>, ASV)). OpenGNT and the local Textus Receptus both have Christ (<em><span lang=\"grc\">μηδε εκπειραζωμεν τον χριστον</span></em> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"1Cor.10.9\">1 Cor 10:9</span>, TR 1894)) and the KJV follows them, \"Neither let us tempt Christ\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"1Cor.10.9\">1 Cor 10:9</span>, KJV); the ASV has the Lord, \"Neither let us make trial of the Lord\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"1Cor.10.9\">1 Cor 10:9</span>, ASV), and the <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">和合本</span> notes the reading, <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">「也不要試探主〔有古卷作：基督〕」（</span><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"1Cor.10.9\">1 Cor 10:9</span><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">，和合本）</span>. The witnesses are to be checked in NA28's apparatus.</li>\n<li>Whose glory? <em><span lang=\"grc\">Ταῦτα εἶπεν Ἠσαΐας ὅτι εἶδεν τὴν δόξαν αὐτοῦ καὶ ἐλάλησεν περὶ αὐτοῦ</span></em> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"John.12.41\">John 12:41</span>, OpenGNT) (the Textus Receptus has <em><span lang=\"grc\">οτε ειδεν</span></em> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"John.12.41\">John 12:41</span>, TR 1894), 'when he saw', as the KJV does; OpenGNT and the ASV 'because'), after quoting <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.10\">Isa 6:10</span> at John 12:40. The surrounding verses speak of Jesus (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"John.12.37\">John 12:37</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"John.12.42\">42</span>), so the pronoun is read of him. <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.1\">Isa 6:1</span> in the LXX used here has glory where the Hebrew has the skirts of the robe (section 6), and this may lie behind John's word; that link is an inference.</li>\n<li>The cry in Revelation. <em><span lang=\"grc\">Ἅγιος ἅγιος ἅγιος Κύριος ὁ Θεός ὁ Παντοκράτωρ</span></em> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rev.4.8\">Rev 4:8</span>, OpenGNT), spoken by the living creatures, each of whom has six wings. <span lang=\"grc\">ζῷον</span> stands 20 times in that book. The local cross-reference data links <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.2\">Isa 6:2</span> with <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rev.4.5-8\">Rev 4:5–8</span>. John does not use the word seraphim, and ISBE calls the living ones 'the only possible equivalent' in the New Testament. Their relation to the seraphim is debated (section 10).</li>\n<li>Early Christian use, outside the canon: 1 Clement 34 (Lightfoot-Harmer's translation, 1891) has 'Holy, holy, holy is the Lord of Sabaoth' followed by 'all creation is full of His glory'. There all creation stands where Isaiah has the whole earth.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"9-culture-and-background-文化背景\">9. Culture and background <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">文化背景</span> <a class=\"up\" href=\"#1-at-a-glance-%E4%B8%80%E8%A6%BD\" title=\"回到摘要 back to the summary\">↑</a></h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Snakes of the wilderness. The wandering is in the area of Edom (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.4\">Num 21:4</span>). Keil says the name comes from 'burning snakes, so called from their burning, i.e. inflammatory bite, which filled with heat and poison', and 'not from the skin of these snakes with fiery red spots', and a footnote gives the traveller von Schubert's account of such a snake in the region. UBS identifies the snake as the 'carpet viper'. The local data cannot tell which species is meant.</li>\n<li>A flying snake? BDB glosses <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.14.29\">Isa 14:29</span> and 30:6 as 'a flying serpent, or dragon', and Delitzsch translates 'adders and flying dragons'. Delitzsch calls it a beast 'of the popular mythology, although Herodotus (ii. 75) speaks of winged serpents in Egypt and Arabia'. <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.30.6\">Isa 30:6</span> places the creature on the route through the Negeb to Egypt, among real animals (lion, viper), in an oracle that is poetry; the local sources do not decide whether a known snake is meant, or a legendary one. Delitzsch reads the oracle of the Davidic house, with the basilisk as Hezekiah and 'the flying dragon is the Messiah', which he gives as the explanation of the Targum. The Targum itself was not consulted here.</li>\n<li>Winged serpents in art. A winged cobra, the uraeus, is an Egyptian royal and divine emblem, and four-winged examples occur on Hebrew seals of the eighth century. K. R. Joines, <em>Winged Serpents in Isaiah's Inaugural Vision</em> (JBL 86, 1967), used this material for Isa 6, and T. N. D. Mettinger surveys the view in the <em>Dictionary of Deities and Demons in the Bible</em>. See the debate in section 10.</li>\n<li>Foreign names. ISBE: 'A Bab name for the fire-god, Nergal, was Sharrapu'. BDB, citing DlWB (Friedrich Delitzsch's Assyrian dictionary) and Zimmern, has Assyrian Šarrapu as an 'epithet of god Nergal', and reports that Dillmann, Marti and others 'compare also Egyptian guardian-griffins', called Šerref. A shared sound does not show a shared origin, and Isa 6 has no hint of a fire-god. ISBE says 'there is nothing of the popular legend or superstition' in the chapter.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"10-theology-神學\">10. Theology <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">神學</span> <a class=\"up\" href=\"#1-at-a-glance-%E4%B8%80%E8%A6%BD\" title=\"回到摘要 back to the summary\">↑</a></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The name: burning ones? The debated point.</strong> (a) From the verb to burn: Strong's derives it so, BDB thinks the second noun 'probably akin to' the first, and F. Delitzsch on Isa 6 (in the Keil-Delitzsch commentary): 'In any case the name seraphim includes the idea of burning'. The common English gloss burning ones rests on this; no verse explains the name. (b) Exalted: Smith's Dictionary reports Gesenius's connection with 'an Arabic term signifying high or exalted', calls it the etymology generally received in its day, and calls the meaning of the word 'extremely doubtful'. (c) Borrowed: the Assyrian and Egyptian comparisons (section 9). ISBE: 'The origin of the term in Heb is uncertain'.</li>\n<li><strong>Winged serpents? The debated point.</strong> BDB says of the second noun that it refers to beings 'originally mythically conceived' with the bodies of serpents, and mentions T. K. Cheyne's alternative, 'personified of lightning'. Delitzsch reports Cheyne, with Riehm, as finding 'serpent-like lightning'. Delitzsch himself reasons from the same noun in Num 21 and <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.14.29\">Isa 14:29</span> that, 'apart from what was human shaped in them', they are 'necessarily to be represented as winged dragons'. Smith reports Hitzig's linking of the name with Sarapis and rejects it, because Sarapis was unknown before Ptolemy Soter. Joines (section 9) argues for the Egyptian winged cobra. Against any close identification: Isa 6 gives faces, feet, wings, a hand and speech, and says nothing of serpent form; ISBE: 'These seraphim are august beings whose forms are not at all fully described'. ISBE reports the view that the seraphim stood for flashes of lightning in popular fancy, and adds that 'none of this appears in' the vision. In favour of a link: Isaiah alone uses both senses and uses the same flying verb of both (section 7).</li>\n<li><strong>Above him, or above it?</strong> The Hebrew is <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">מִ מַּ֨עַל֙ לֹ֔ו</span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.2\">Isa 6:2</span>, BHSA), and the pronoun may refer to the Lord or to the throne. The ASV has \"Above him stood the seraphim\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.2\">Isa 6:2</span>, ASV); the KJV \"Above it stood the seraphims\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.2\">Isa 6:2</span>, KJV); the LXX used here round about (section 6); the <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">和合本 「其上」（</span><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.2\">Isa 6:2</span><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">，和合本）</span>, which keeps it open. Smith reads above Jehovah, against the above it of the KJV. Delitzsch applies the pronoun to him, takes the phrase literally, and reports that the Targum and Rashi explain it 'above, for His service'. He pictures them hovering, in 'two semicircular choirs'.</li>\n<li><strong>Seraphim and cherubim. The debated point.</strong> Delitzsch: 'seraphim and cherubim are heavenly beings, different in kind'. Setting <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.6\">Isa 6:6</span> beside <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ezek.10.6-7\">Ezek 10:6–7</span>, he contrasts them in this way: the seraphim are 'the bearers and mediators of the fire of divine love', and the cherubim of Ezekiel 'the bearers and mediators of the fire of divine wrath'. The wings differ: at <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.2\">Isa 6:2</span> two cover the face and two the feet, and at <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ezek.1.11\">Ezek 1:11</span> \"two wings of every one were joined one to another, and two covered their bodies\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ezek.1.11\">Ezek 1:11</span>, ASV). Revelation's living creatures combine features of both, and the attempts to identify them have, Delitzsch says, 'only an apparent support' there. ISBE says that in Jewish theology seraphim, cherubim and ophanim stand 'as the three highest orders of attendants on Jeh', and Delitzsch reports the nine choirs from Dionysius the Areopagite (see S25). No biblical passage lists these ranks.</li>\n<li><strong>Three times holy.</strong> Hastings' <em>Dictionary of Christ and the Gospels</em>, vol. 2 (1908), on the number three: the song and the priestly blessing 'cannot be safely' read as 'foreshadowings of the doctrine of the Trinity', and the number is emphatic. Delitzsch describes it as 'an antiphonal song proceeding without interruption'. Christian writers have widely heard the Trinity in it and in \"who will go for us?\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.8\">Isa 6:8</span>, ASV). The Great Isaiah Scroll from Qumran (1QIsa-a, in the ETCBC edition by Abegg, Bowley and Cook) has the word twice at <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.3\">Isa 6:3</span>: <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">קדוש קדוש יהוה צבאות</span>, with the plural participle <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">וקראים</span>. The Masoretic Text and the LXX used here have it three times, and the Greek of <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rev.4.8\">Rev 4:8</span> too.</li>\n<li><strong>The coal, and who forgives.</strong> BDB glosses <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">רִצְפָּה</span> as 'glowing stone' (with coal as the alternative); Delitzsch rejects the meaning stone, as part of a pavement, as 'erroneously adopted by Aquila and others'. The Hebrew passive <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">תְּכֻפָּֽר׃</span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.7\">Isa 6:7</span>, BHSA) names no agent, and the seraph announces the forgiveness and is not said to grant it. ISBE: 'One of them is the agent for burning' the sin from the prophet's lips, 'with a coal off the altar', and not, it adds, by the seraph's power or person. Delitzsch nevertheless calls the act 'a heavenly sacrament' and 'a seraphic absolution', with the fire burning 'not in a destructive way, but in a wholesome way'. What matters in the text: the coal comes from the altar, and the prophet is forgiven before he is sent (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.7-8\">Isa 6:7–8</span>).</li>\n<li><strong>The bronze serpent: sign or remedy?</strong> Keil: it was 'a sign that the deadly poison of the fiery serpents was overcome in this brazen serpent', and not 'a symbol of the divine healing power', which he says against Winer, Kurtz and Knobel, reading it with Wisd 16:6–7 as 'a symbol of salvation' and the look as faith in God's promise. He also says brass was chosen because its colour 'was most like the appearance of the fiery serpents'. Delitzsch, comparing <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.6-9\">Num 21:6–9</span> with the seraph's coal, says of the elevated serpent that from it 'there proceeds a healing power which makes the deadly poison ineffective'. The text says only that whoever looked lived (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.8-9\">Num 21:8–9</span>), and <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"John.3.14\">John 3:14</span> draws one comparison, the lifting up.</li>\n<li>Where the traditions differ (one neutral line): all confess a holy God attended by spirits he made; the nine choirs of Dionysius, with seraphim first, shaped Orthodox and medieval Catholic teaching, while many Protestants, Calvin among them, declined to speculate on the ranks. The data do not settle this.</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"12-for-practice-應用\">11. For practice <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">應用</span> <a class=\"up\" href=\"#1-at-a-glance-%E4%B8%80%E8%A6%BD\" title=\"回到摘要 back to the summary\">↑</a></h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Teaching slide: <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שָׂרָף</span> = <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">撒拉弗</span> in Isa 6, <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">火蛇</span> in Numbers, <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">火焰的飛龍</span> in Isaiah's oracles. One Hebrew noun, seven times, three kinds of Chinese. What the seraphim do: stand, cover, fly, call, carry a coal, speak.</li>\n<li>Bible class: read <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.1-8\">Isa 6:1–8</span> aloud, then <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.4-9\">Num 21:4–9</span> with <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"John.3.14-15\">John 3:14–15</span> and <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"2Kgs.18.4\">2 Kgs 18:4</span>. Ask what each text says about looking, and how a God-given sign became an object of worship.</li>\n<li>Personal: \"because I am a man of unclean lips\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.5\">Isa 6:5</span>, ASV) comes before the commission. The sequence in the chapter is sight, confession, cleansing, then sending.</li>\n<li>For preachers: the seraphim are mentioned in a single chapter. They are not a subject for speculation about angelic rank, and the chapter's own weight falls on the Lord who sits on the throne and on the sinner who is forgiven.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">連在一起</span> Connected</strong> — how they relate · why it matters · how we live it out:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bible truths <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">聖經真理</span>:</strong> <span class=\"off\">30 <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">神是聖潔的</span></span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.3\">Isa 6:3</span>) · <span class=\"off\">93 <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">天使是受造的</span></span> · <span class=\"off\">94 <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">服役的靈</span></span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.2-3\">Isa 6:2–3</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.6-7\">6–7</span>) · <span class=\"off\">96 <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">基督超乎天使</span></span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"John.12.41\">John 12:41</span>) · the written lesson <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#i9a03ac51dc\">Lesson 3 <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">一次獻上</span></a> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"John.3.14\">John 3:14</span>) · the lifted-up Son. <span class=\"off\">WST D32</span> treats angels for training.</li>\n<li><strong>Book studies <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">書卷研究</span>:</strong> <span class=\"off\">John</span> (3:14; 12:41) · the lifting and the glory Isaiah saw · <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#i7a592efe93\">Hebrews</a> (1:7) · the ministers of flame · <span class=\"off\">Revelation</span> (4:8) · six wings and the same cry.</li>\n<li><strong>World Watch <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">世界觀察</span>:</strong> <span class=\"off\">the Dead Sea Scrolls</span> · 1QIsa-a at <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.3\">Isa 6:3</span>, the cry with two holies (section 10).</li>\n<li><strong>Church history <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">教會歷史</span>:</strong> <span class=\"off\">the church history branch</span> · Dionysius' nine choirs and the later dispute over them (section 10).</li>\n<li><strong>Hymns <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">聖詩</span>:</strong> <span class=\"off\">Holy, Holy, Holy <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">聖哉聖哉聖哉</span></span> · cherubim and seraphim in the worship of Rev 4 · for a Lord's Day on Isa 6 or Rev 4.</li>\n<li><strong>The Lampstand 燈臺: </strong>knop 1, the Word of God · knop 2, proclaimed: the prophet sent at <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.8\">Isa 6:8</span>.</li>\n<li><strong>Word studies <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">原文研究</span>:</strong> <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#ifc420ed831\">S24 angel</a> (the larger family of heavenly beings; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Heb.1.7\">Heb 1:7</span>) · <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#i86d7c9abae\">S25 cherubim</a> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ezek.10.7\">Ezek 10:7</span>; the other named class) · <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#i45a4481517\">S3 glory</a> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.3\">Isa 6:3</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"John.12.41\">John 12:41</span>) · <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#i61e4203df1\">S4 holy</a> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.3\">Isa 6:3</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rev.4.8\">Rev 4:8</span>) · <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#ib9021d39fb\">S10 high priest</a> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Lev.16.12\">Lev 16:12</span>) · <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#i3adf5d2ccf\">S17 God</a> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.1\">Isa 6:1</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.5\">5</span>) · <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#i8d544e8b31\">S15 repentance and falling away</a> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.5\">Isa 6:5</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.7\">7</span>).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">標籤詞</span> Tag words</strong> — <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">中文</span> · English · Hebrew · Greek (the shared vocabulary; counts = words in the text; a Hebrew or Greek word opens its word study, or its lexicon entry where no study exists yet):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>#t/seraphim <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">撒拉弗</span> · seraphim · <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#idac416b214\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שָׂרָף</span></a> H8314 (7) · <span class=\"off\"><span lang=\"grc\">σεραφίμ</span></span> G9067 (LXX 2)</li>\n<li>#t/serpent <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">蛇</span> · serpent · <span class=\"off\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">נָחָשׁ</span></span> H5175 (31) · <span class=\"off\"><span lang=\"grc\">ὄφις</span></span> G3789 (14)</li>\n<li>#t/holy <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">聖</span> · holy · <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#i61e4203df1\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">קָדוֹשׁ</span></a> H6918 (118) · <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#i61e4203df1\"><span lang=\"grc\">ἅγιος</span></a> G40 (233)</li>\n<li>#t/fire <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">火</span> · fire · <span class=\"off\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">אֵשׁ</span></span> H784 (379) · <span class=\"off\"><span lang=\"grc\">πῦρ</span></span> G4442 (71)</li>\n<li>#t/atonement <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">贖罪</span> · atonement · <span class=\"off\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">כָּפַר</span></span> H3722 (104)</li>\n<li>#t/glory <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">榮耀</span> · glory · <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#i45a4481517\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">כָּבוֹד</span></a> H3519 (200) · <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#i45a4481517\"><span lang=\"grc\">δόξα</span></a> G1391 (166)</li>\n<li>#t/angel <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">天使</span> · angel · <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#ifc420ed831\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">מַלְאָךְ</span></a> H4397 (213) · <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#ifc420ed831\"><span lang=\"grc\">ἄγγελος</span></a> G32 (175)</li>\n<li>#t/cherubim <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">基路伯</span> · cherubim · <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#i86d7c9abae\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">כְּרוּב</span></a> H3742 (91) · <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#i86d7c9abae\"><span lang=\"grc\">χερουβίμ</span></a> G5502 (1)</li>\n<li>#t/living-creatures <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">活物</span> · living creatures · <span class=\"off\"><span lang=\"grc\">ζῶον</span></span> G2226 (23)</li>\n<li>#t/prophet <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">先知</span> · prophet · <span class=\"off\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">נָבִיא</span></span> H5030 (316) · <span class=\"off\"><span lang=\"grc\">προφήτης</span></span> G4396 (144)</li>\n<li>grammar <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">文法</span>: <span class=\"off\">the endings <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">詞尾</span> (plural <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שְׂרָפִ֨ים</span>, Isa 6:2; dual <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">כְּנָפַ֛יִם</span>, Isa 6:2)</span> (<span class=\"off\">page 1 <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">圖</span></span>) · <span class=\"off\">suffixes on nouns <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">名詞詞尾</span> (<span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">פָנָ֗יו</span>, Isa 6:2)</span> (<span class=\"off\">page 4 <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">圖</span></span>) · <span class=\"off\">Piel <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">加強</span> (<span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">יְכַסֶּ֣ה</span>, Isa 6:2)</span> (<span class=\"off\">page 2 <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">圖</span></span>) · <span class=\"off\">Pual (<span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">תְּכֻפָּֽר</span>, Isa 6:7)</span> (<span class=\"off\">page 2 <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">圖</span></span>) · <span class=\"off\">construct chain <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">附屬鏈</span> (<span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">נְחַ֣שׁ נְחֹ֔שֶׁת</span>, Num 21:9)</span> (<span class=\"off\">page 4 <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">圖</span></span>)</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"13-evaluation-評估\">12. Evaluation <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">評估</span> <a class=\"up\" href=\"#1-at-a-glance-%E4%B8%80%E8%A6%BD\" title=\"回到摘要 back to the summary\">↑</a></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Why it matters:</strong> one noun holds together a judgment of snakes, a God-given sign, and the beings before the holy God who bring a sinner fire from the altar. </li>\n<li><strong>Where it is strongest:</strong> <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.6\">Num 21:6</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.8\">8</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Deut.8.15\">Deut 8:15</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.2\">Isa 6:2</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.6\">6</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.14.29\">14:29</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.30.6\">30:6</span>, and the surrounding text of <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.1-8\">Isa 6:1–8</span> and <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.4-9\">Num 21:4–9</span>.</li>\n<li><strong>For whom:</strong> the congregation (holiness, confession, sending), new believers (what seraphim are and are not), the Bible class (the two senses and what can be known).</li>\n<li><strong>Limits:</strong> the study is short because the evidence is short. Seven occurrences give the forms, translations and contexts. They do not decide the word's origin, whether winged serpents stand behind it, how seraphim relate to cherubim or the living creatures, or their rank; the positions are stated with their sources.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"14-sources-and-to-check-資料與待查\">13. Sources 資料 <a class=\"up\" href=\"#1-at-a-glance-%E4%B8%80%E8%A6%BD\" title=\"回到摘要 back to the summary\">↑</a></h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Texts and tags: BHSA (ETCBC), OpenGNT 3.3, the chart <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">舊約在新約</span> The Old Testament in the New with OpenBible.info verse votes for section 6, eBible KJV 1769 and ASV 1901 with Strong's tags, OpenHebrewBible and OpenGNT <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">和合本</span> alignments, UBS SDBH and UBS Greek NT dictionaries, ISBE (1915).</li>\n<li>Editions: 和合本; KJV 1769; ASV 1901; BHSA (Hebrew); OpenGNT (Greek NT); LXX = the Apostolic Bible Polyglot (the Sixtine of 1587 as reprinted by Bos 1709, not Rahlfs–Hanhart); Textus Receptus = Scrivener 1894 where it is named.</li>\n<li>The LXX used here (the Apostolic Bible Polyglot) is the Sixtine of 1587 via Bos 1709, not Rahlfs-Hanhart. Every LXX line here (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.6\">Num 21:6</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.21.8\">8</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Deut.8.15\">Deut 8:15</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.1\">Isa 6:1</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.2\">2</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.3\">3</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.6\">6</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.7\">7</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.14.29\">14:29</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.30.6\">30:6</span>) is that text;</li>\n\n<li>Dead Sea Scrolls: the reading of 1QIsa-a at <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.2-3\">Isa 6:2–3</span> (column 5) comes from the ETCBC DSS 2.0 data (Abegg, Bowley, Cook), and the words cited there stand outside the editors' square brackets. In 4Q55 at <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.6.6\">Isa 6:6</span>, the word <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שרפים</span> stands inside those brackets, so that copy is no witness to it. 1 Clement 34 is Lightfoot-Harmer's 1891 translation.</li>\n\n<li>Textual questions: <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"1Cor.10.9\">1 Cor 10:9</span> has Lord or Christ. At <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.14.29\">Isa 14:29</span> the <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">和合本</span> pronoun before <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">所生</span> is <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">他</span> in the printing used here and <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">牠</span> in another printing, a difference of printing, so only <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">是火焰的飛龍</span> is quoted here.</li>\n\n</ul>\n<details class=\"pn across\" open><summary>跨燈 <i lang=\"en\">Across the lamps</i></summary><p class=\"acr\" data-b=\"1\"></p></details>"}});
